File Index

/crontab-root
Purpose: This crontab entry automates nginx log rotation every 15 minutes using logrotate utility.

/edge-subdomains.conf
Purpose: Configures nginx routing and proxy settings for multiple subdomains and services. Phase F: added a kingdom-tactics.shatteredarchive.dev server block (single subdomain for BOTH kingdom-tactics-client and -server — /api/kt/, /ws/kt (WebSocket upgrade), and /health proxy to the server; everything else to the client), mirroring the build.shatteredarchive.dev/auth.shatteredarchive.dev pattern, not a separate client/server subdomain pair. Live-verified via Host-header-override curl/http.request calls (no browser available in this environment) through the real recreated nginx container — every other subdomain regression-checked in the same pass. Scrum Poker (2026-07-28): added a scrum-poker.shatteredarchive.dev block on the same single-subdomain, resolver+variable pattern — /api/scrum/, /ws/scrum (WebSocket, proxy_read_timeout 3600 for long quiet stretches between stories) and /health to scrum-poker-server:63000, everything else to the SPA. Verified by running the REAL extracted block in a throwaway nginx container with the real images network-aliased as scrum-poker-server/-client: nginx -t clean, and all four routes plus a 101 upgrade exercised end-to-end without touching the live stack. 2026-07-29: added an ACTIVE real_ip block at the top — set_real_ip_from ${FRONT_PROXY_CIDR} / real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For / real_ip_recursive on. Load-bearing for the limit_* zones: they key on $binary_remote_addr, which behind the front proxy (DSL/nginx/shattered_archive.site) is that proxy's single address for ALL internet traffic, silently turning the per-IP limits into GLOBAL ones (10 room creations/min for the whole world, 128 total websockets — two teams would knock each other offline). FRONT_PROXY_CIDR is a REQUIRED env var supplied by the nginx service in BOTH compose files as "${FRONT_PROXY_CIDR:-144.217.6.225/32}" and substituted by the entrypoint's envsubst pass, same mechanism as ${NGINX_TLS_INCLUDE_FILE} — if it is ever unset, envsubst leaves the literal ${...} behind and nginx REFUSES TO START, so override it via env, never by editing this file. Must stay a /32 of the proxy itself: anything trusted here can forge X-Forwarded-For and skip the limits. Inert on the experimental stack (nothing reaches it via the front proxy). Verified in-container: rewrite fires only for a trusted source, and a forged XFF prefix still resolves to the real appended client because the front proxy appends and recursive walks right-to-left. 2026-07-29 (CodeQL "missing rate limiting"): extended the same edge-limits approach from scrum to auth + the builder — three new $binary_remote_addr zones (auth_login 30r/m, auth_api 300r/m, builder_api 600r/m), applied as auth.shatteredarchive.dev's new /api/auth/ block (tighter: the unauthenticated login/signup/forgot/reset surface, which also sends mail; a LONGER prefix than /api/ so nginx matches it first) plus limit_req on auth's and build's /api/. All return 429 rather than nginx's default 503. Deliberately chose the edge over an in-app limiter: the apps only ever see nginx's address, and auth-server's LoginLockout counts FAILED passwords by username+IP, not request volume. Sizing is generous on purpose (shared-NAT teams; the builder's dashboards fan out dozens of reads per page load) — these stop credential stuffing and scraping, not normal use. Service-to-service traffic is NOT charged: AUTH_SERVER_URL is the internal alias auth-server.shatteredarchive.dev:62000, so other containers' introspect/token-exchange calls never transit the edge. Note CodeQL will NOT close those alerts from this — it only recognizes in-app limiter middleware, so they need dismissing as handled at the edge. Verified with a real `nginx -t` through the entrypoint's envsubst pass in BOTH NGINX_TLS_INCLUDE_FILE modes. Same pass fixed a PRE-EXISTING body-size misalignment found while adding those limits: nothing here or above ever set client_max_body_size, so build's /api/ inherited nginx's built-in 1m default while mud-builder-server's /api/import and /api/snippets accept 2mb — a large .are import was already failing at the edge with nginx's HTML 413 before reaching the app. Now 3m, deliberately ABOVE the app's largest cap rather than equal to it, so express keeps the exact per-route limits and the clean JSON errors and nginx stays a coarse outer guard. auth needs no equivalent: its largest express limit is 64kb, well under the 1m default, and its /api/auth/ block pins 64k explicitly. Ads (2026-08-05): the scrum-poker block's CSP include became env-switched — `include /etc/nginx/includes/${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE};` — because it is the ONLY app permitted to carry a Google ad unit and an ad-permitting CSP costs 'unsafe-inline' in script-src, which must not be paid on a build with empty ad args. REQUIRED env var like the other two (unset ⇒ literal ${...} ⇒ nginx refuses to start); prod compose sets security-headers-ads.conf, experimental sets security-headers.conf. The variable works ONLY in this file: the entrypoint runs envsubst over /etc/nginx/templates/, not over the bind-mounted includes/, so the HTTPS mirror in includes/tls-dev.conf had to name its profile literally — a first attempt at sharing the variable there failed `nginx -t` with `open() ".../${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE}" failed`. Both stack configurations re-verified with a real `nginx -t` through the entrypoint's envsubst pass. robots.txt (2026-08-05): every app/API block gained `include .../robots-disallow-all.conf` and scrum-poker `include .../robots-scrum-poker.conf` — mirrored into includes/tls-dev.conf per the usual both-schemes rule. Done as shared includes rather than inline per block because there are eight of them across two schemes and drift would be invisible. Necessary, not cosmetic: without an exact-match location, /robots.txt falls through `location /` to a container whose conf does `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html`, so a crawler receives the app's HTML with a 200 and treats the host as having no rules at all. The apex shatteredarchive.dev is deliberately NOT given one here — it never reaches this edge (the front proxy serves it from a static docroot) and it is the one host that SHOULD be crawlable, so its robots.txt is inline in DSL/nginx/shattered_archive.site alongside ads.txt.

/edge.conf
Purpose: This file configures nginx to route HTTPS traffic to different upstream services based on the hostname.

/entrypoint.sh
Purpose: This script initializes NGINX server with self-signed TLS certificates and configures log rotation for the development environment.

/game-client.conf
Purpose: This file configures the Nginx server settings for the game client web application.

/kingdom-tactics-client.conf
Purpose: Phase F — per-container nginx conf baked into the kingdom-tactics-client image (SPA static serve, try_files fallback to index.html). Identical shape to mud-builder-client.conf; the edge subdomain routing lives in edge-subdomains.conf, not here.

/logrotate-nginx.conf
Purpose: Configures daily rotation and compression of nginx log files with 14-day retention.

/scrum-poker-client.conf
Purpose: Per-container nginx conf baked into the scrum-poker-client image (SPA static serve, try_files fallback to index.html). Same shape as mud-builder-client.conf, but the fallback is load-bearing here rather than incidental: room URLs are real paths (/room/3f2504e0-4f89-41d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301) meant to be pasted into chat and opened cold, so a direct hit must serve index.html rather than 404.

/shatteredarchive-dev-stub.conf
Purpose: This configuration file sets up nginx server settings for the shatteredarchive-dev development environment.

/ssl-params.conf
Purpose: Configures SSL parameters and security headers for local development with self-signed certificates.

/web-client.conf
Purpose: This file configures the Nginx web server for the client-side application.
